# Where a DO/LOOP header's clause boundaries fall, which is the axis a
# flattened header can get wrong and no shape case asks about.
#
# A loop header has more boundaries than a branch does, and they are not the
# same event:
#
#   * the DO clause's own, which the header's expressions run inside;
#   * one per pass for the re-test, whose clause is whichever instruction
#     transferred control back to the loop -- the DO on the first pass, the END
#     on a fall-through, an ITERATE that cut a pass short;
#   * UNTIL's own, which is a separate decision point from the top-of-loop one.
#
# Each row queues a CALL ON handler from one of those places and observes where
# it is delivered, because a promoted clause that opens a boundary and does not
# discharge it is invisible to every test that only asks what a loop computes.
#
# Two rows are about a boundary leaving MORE work behind it. A delivered
# handler can itself RAISE ... RETURN, queueing a second trap, and a clause
# boundary delivers at most one without re-checking -- so the boundary that
# delivered the first is not the last one with work, and the SIGL of the second
# delivery is what says which boundary took it.
#
# Three rows are about the header's own ordering rather than its boundaries,
# and they are here because the compiled form is what makes the ordering a
# thing that can break: each header value is evaluated, echoed and validated
# before the next value is evaluated at all.
#
# EVERY EXPECTED BYTE BELOW WAS MEASURED AGAINST THE C++ ORACLE, and every row
# was measured to produce the identical bytes on both engines at `de05ea58`,
# before the header was flattened -- which is what says a row can tell whether
# it would ever have failed. One row differs from the oracle and says so in its
# own comment. Regenerate a row by running its program under the oracle, never
# with REWRITE=1 -- ir_dual.rs refuses to run at all under REWRITE for that
# reason. Capture is:
#
#   ( ulimit -v 1048576; LD_LIBRARY_PATH=<ooRexx>/build/lib \
#     <ooRexx>/build/bin/rexx PROGRAM.rex </dev/null )
#
# TWO LOOP-HEADER BOUNDARIES DIVERGE FROM THE ORACLE AND ARE NOT ROWS HERE,
# because a row's expected block is what this crate prints and these two are
# places where that is not what the oracle prints. Both were measured on both
# engines, and the engines agree on each; neither moved when the header was
# flattened.
#
#   * **A zero-pass loop whose requeued handler has no body clause behind it.**
#     `do i = 1 to raiser()` with `raiser` returning 0 and its handler raising a
#     second trapped condition: the oracle prints `after` and then `G ran 6`,
#     delivering the second trap at the next real clause, and this crate prints
#     `G ran 3` and then `after`, delivering it at the `DO` clause's own boundary.
#     The `DO` clause encloses the whole loop here, so its boundary runs after
#     the loop rather than before the body -- and with zero passes there is no
#     body clause in between to take the delivery instead. The row above with one
#     pass reports `G ran 4` on both sides, which is why this shape needs its own
#     mention rather than being covered by it.
#   * **An `UNTIL` requeue.** The oracle delivers the second trap at the `END`'s
#     own clause, `G ran 5`; this crate delivers it at the clause after the loop,
#     `G ran 6`. The `END` is an instruction the oracle executes and this crate
#     never steps -- it echoes it and jumps past it.
#
# Both are the elided-instruction family `task-4bp-report.md` catalogues: the
# oracle runs a clause boundary at a position where this crate has no instruction
# to run one at. They are recorded here rather than in a report because this is
# the file the next reader of a loop-header boundary opens.
#
# Expected lines are tagged so that no line of the block can be empty: an empty
# line ends an expected block, and a program's own output may hold one.
#
# `/nonexistent/ir-dual-case.rex` is `ir_dual.rs`'s own INLINE_PATH, which a
# raised condition's middle line prints. It is written out rather than stood in
# for, because these blocks are compared verbatim and nothing substitutes into
# them; running the same program under the oracle prints the oracle's own path
# there instead.

# a call on handler queued by a TO expression
# The handler is queued while the header is being evaluated and has to run
# before the first body clause reads the variable it sets -- both passes print
# `set`. A header whose clause never ends would print `unset` twice.
program
call on user zx name h
zv = 'unset'
do i = 1 to raiser()
  say 'body' zv
end
say 'after' zv
exit
raiser:
raise user zx return 2
h:
zv = 'set'
return
----
rc> 0
out> body set
out> body set
out> after set

# a handler queued by a TO expression that queues again
# The premise a single boundary per construct gets wrong: `h` runs at the
# header's boundary and its own RAISE queues `zy`, which nothing at that same
# boundary delivers. SIGL 4 is the first body clause, so the second delivery is
# the *next* boundary rather than the header's own -- and a header that
# delivered both would report 3.
program
call on user zx name h
call on user zy name g
do i = 1 to raiser()
  nop
end
say 'after'
exit
raiser:
raise user zx return 1
h:
raise user zy return 1
g:
say 'G ran' sigl
return
----
rc> 0
out> G ran 4
out> after

# a call on handler failing at a DO header's own boundary
# The failure the handler raises is attributed to the clause whose boundary ran
# it, which is the DO's own -- the defect one construct over was a promoted IF
# recording its own failure site but not its boundary's.
#
# **The handler's own line is the oracle's; the enclosing frame's is not.**
# The oracle prints `     2 *-*   do i = 1 to raiser()` for the frame the
# handler was delivered from, two columns further in, because its traceback
# reads each activation's live indent counter and the `DO` block is still open
# there. This crate reports that frame at the `DO` clause's own recorded
# failure site instead, which carries no block level. That is a traceback
# quantity rather than a transfer one -- the line above it, the handler's own
# clause, agrees byte for byte.
#
# **A `SELECT CASE` diverges the same way, in the same single line.** Measured
# on both engines, `trace r` / `call on user zx name h` / `select case
# raiser()` with a handler that divides by zero -- so `select case` is line 3
# -- the enclosing frame's traceback line:
#
#   oracle       3 *-*   select case raiser()
#   this crate   3 *-* select case raiser()
#
# The handler's own two lines above it agree byte for byte, so this is the same
# family and the same reason: the `SELECT`'s block is still open where the
# oracle's traceback reads the counter, and this crate's recorded failure site
# carries no block level.
#
# **A `SELECT CASE`'s clause boundary is the same rule one construct over.**
# `SELECT` is a block instruction too, so the oracle's counter is already one
# deeper when that clause ends, and `Interp::settle_block_indent` is what both
# constructs reach. A trap queued by a `WHEN` condition settles at the level
# its clause echoed at instead, because a `WHEN` is not a block instruction.
# Both are held against the oracle's own bytes in `tests/trace_indent.rs`.
#
# **A plain `SELECT`'s own boundary is still two columns short, and it is this
# same defect on the construct that has no `CASE`.** A boundary delivers
# whatever is pending rather than only what its own clause queued: a `CALL ON`
# handler ending in `raise ... return` leaves a second condition for the next
# boundary to take, and a plain `select` standing there is what takes it.
# Measured under `trace r` on both engines, `SIGL` naming the `select` clause's
# own line, so it is that boundary delivering:
#
#   oracle       15 *-*     g:
#   this crate   15 *-*   g:
#
# `select case 1` is the same program with a scrutinee that queues nothing
# either, and it agrees, because `Interp::select_case` settles the boundary and
# a caseless `SELECT` never reaches it. **So what decides is whether anything
# settled the boundary, never what the clause queued** -- which is why "its
# clause queues nothing" is not a reason to leave a block instruction's
# boundary unsettled.
#
# **A DIFFERENT FAMILY, AND THE ONE NOT TO FILE BESIDE THESE.** The same
# requeue route pointed at a plain `do` / `end` is not an indent and not a
# trace quantity: the oracle delivers the second handler at the `do` clause's
# own boundary, before the body runs, and this crate runs the body's first
# clause first and delivers at that clause's boundary instead. Wrong order and
# a different `SIGL`, on **stdout**, with no `TRACE` anywhere in the program --
# which is what makes it worse than everything else recorded here rather than
# another entry on the list:
#
#   oracle       G ran 5 / body / after set 5
#   this crate   body / G ran 6 / after set 5
#
# `do label zl` behaves the same and `if ... then` is clean on this route. It
# belongs to the elided-boundary family this file's header block already
# describes -- the oracle runs a clause boundary at a position where this crate
# has no instruction to run one at -- and not to the indent quantities above.
program
call on user zx name h
do i = 1 to raiser()
  nop
end
say 'after'
exit
raiser:
raise user zx return 1
h:
say 1/0
return
----
rc> 214
err>     10 *-*     say 1/0
err>      2 *-* do i = 1 to raiser()
err> Error 42 running /nonexistent/ir-dual-case.rex line 10:  Arithmetic overflow/underflow.
err> Error 42.3:  Arithmetic overflow; divisor must not be zero.

# a call on handler queued by a WHILE condition
# A different boundary from the header's: the re-test before pass 2 is its own
# clause, and the handler it queues runs before that pass's body. So pass 1
# reads `unset` and pass 2 reads `set`, which no single delivery point produces.
program
call on user zx name h
zn = 0
zv = 'unset'
do while zn < 2 & cond()
  zn = zn + 1
  say 'pass' zn zv
end
say 'after' zv
exit
cond:
if zn = 1 then raise user zx return 1
return 1
h:
zv = 'set'
return
----
rc> 0
out> pass 1 unset
out> pass 2 set
out> after set

# a handler queued by a WHILE condition that queues again
# The re-queue at the per-pass boundary rather than at the header's. `zn` is 0
# when `cond` raises, so this is the *first* test, whose clause is the DO's own
# -- and SIGL 5 is the END, the clause of the boundary that took the second
# delivery. The loop then runs one pass and stops.
program
call on user zx name h
call on user zy name g
zn = 0
do while cond()
  zn = zn + 1
end
say 'after' zn
exit
cond:
if zn = 0 then raise user zx return 1
return 0
h:
raise user zy return 1
g:
say 'G ran' sigl
return
----
rc> 0
out> G ran 5
out> after 1

# a call on handler queued by an UNTIL condition
# UNTIL's own test is a third boundary, and SIGL 5 is the END rather than the
# DO: an UNTIL is reached from whichever instruction transferred control back to
# the loop, and after a pass that fell through that is the END.
program
call on user zx name h
zn = 0
do until done()
  zn = zn + 1
end
say 'after' zn
exit
done:
if zn = 1 then raise user zx return 0
return zn >= 2
h:
say 'H ran' sigl
return
----
rc> 0
out> H ran 5
out> after 2

# a call on handler queued by a DO OVER target
# The same header boundary for the one LoopKind whose header value survives the
# whole construct: the target is bound to the control variable on the single
# pass, and the handler still runs before that pass's body.
program
call on user zx name h
zv = 'unset'
do qq over raiser()
  say 'body' qq zv
end
say 'after' zv
exit
raiser:
raise user zx return 'T'
h:
zv = 'set'
return
----
rc> 0
out> body T set
out> after set

# procedure as a loop body's first instruction
# A loop body does not inherit the first-instruction permission any more than a
# branch does, so this is 17.1 -- the observable that says the header's promoted
# clause spends the permission exactly as the unpromoted instruction did.
program
call sub 1
exit
sub:
  do i = 1 to 1
    procedure
  end
  return
